    "text": "Ingredient library.\nA plain reference to botanicals used in Ayurveda: which plant, which part, and how to tell it from look-alikes. It describes identity only, with no health claims.\nListing a plant here does not mean it will be used in any Kaayra product. Entries are educational and are being reviewed.\nAshwagandha (Indian winter cherry): Withania somnifera (L.) Dunal, family Solanaceae, plant part: Root. A classical Ayurvedic herb. Material is usually sold as dried root powder; the plant part matters, as leaf and root are different materials.\nBrahmi (Water hyssop): Bacopa monnieri (L.) Wettst., family Plantaginaceae, plant part: Aerial parts. The name Brahmi is also applied to Centella asiatica in some regions, so the botanical name is the only reliable way to say which plant is meant.\nAmalaki (Indian gooseberry, amla): Phyllanthus emblica L., family Phyllanthaceae, plant part: Fruit. Older texts and labels may list this plant as Emblica officinalis, a synonym. One of the three fruits in the classical preparation Triphala.\nHaritaki (Chebulic myrobalan): Terminalia chebula Retz., family Combretaceae, plant part: Fruit. One of the three fruits in Triphala. Fruit grade and maturity vary, which is one reason supplier information matters.\nBibhitaki (Beleric myrobalan): Terminalia bellerica (Gaertn.) Roxb., family Combretaceae, plant part: Fruit. One of the three fruits in Triphala. Closely related to Haritaki but a different species.\nGuduchi (Heart-leaved moonseed, giloy): Tinospora cordifolia (Willd.) Hook.f. & Thomson, family Menispermaceae, plant part: Stem. Often sold as giloy. Look-alike Tinospora species exist, so identity confirmation is important.\nTulsi (Holy basil): Ocimum tenuiflorum L., family Lamiaceae, plant part: Leaf. Also written Ocimum sanctum. Several tulsi varieties are grown, which differ in appearance and aroma.\nPunarnava (Spreading hogweed): Boerhavia diffusa L., family Nyctaginaceae, plant part: Root. The name Punarnava is applied to more than one plant in different regions, so botanical identification is essential."
